Hi Ken,
  I agree about not modifying CharsetDetector.  In the mime-type case, it looks like AutoDetectParser returns an EmptyParser via CompositeParser if there is no matching parser for a mime-type. 
  HTMLEncodingDetector, Icu4JEncodingDetector and UniversalEncodingDetector test for supported charsets and will return null if no supported charset is found.  It looks like AutoDetectReader will throw a TikaException if no charset can be identified.
  Would this be acceptable:
{noformat}
if(match.getConfidence() &gt; 35 &amp;&amp; Charset.isSupported(match.getName())) {
    msg.set7BitEncoding( match.getName() );
}
{noformat}  
  This effectively backs off to ASCII.  This could lead to a garbled 7bit encoding, but at least the attachments and metadata would be extracted.  I&apos;m not thrilled with this solution...

